(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell["Exact Stabilizer Codes", "Title",
 CellID->874461601,ExpressionUUID->"22a1a762-10e3-861e-525e-3f4ca097ed64"],

Cell["\<\
Symbolic, sign-exact stabilizer-code analysis in the Wolfram Language, \
re-derived from GF(2) first principles, cross-checked against Stim, and \
carrying a parameter Stim cannot.\
\>", "Subtitle",
 CellID->36768919,ExpressionUUID->"4da796ef-9f57-a4bf-7b5c-0fc91ae30e85"],

Cell["Mads Bahrami (last updated: Aug 24, 2026)", "Author",
 CellID->1699946459,ExpressionUUID->"1b39f900-6440-cee7-4ac6-f16a0dfa94cd"],

Cell["Wolfram Research Inc, USA", "Affiliation",
 CellID->1535504786,ExpressionUUID->"75d93363-5da6-51a4-23e6-ab4d5453238e"],

Cell["Setting the Stage: How This Notebook Flows", "Subsection",
 CellID->1208482393,ExpressionUUID->"25a568a6-87a2-527f-535a-810032572145"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This notebook is a computation-first tour of a small, sharp corner of \
quantum error correction: the ",
 StyleBox["exact", "TI"],
 " analysis of stabilizer codes. For the five textbook codes (the three-qubit \
bit-flip and phase-flip codes, the nine-qubit Shor code, the five-qubit \
perfect code, and the seven-qubit Steane code) we compute the exact code \
distance and a minimum-weight witness for it, the logical Pauli operators \
together with their full commutation algebra, and a sign-exact +1 Clifford \
encoder. Every one of these results is then verified two independent ways: by \
a from-scratch symplectic re-derivation written in this notebook, and by a \
differential comparison against ",
 ButtonBox["Stim",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://github.com/quantumlib/Stim"], None}],
 ", the field-standard stabilizer simulator. At the end we do the one thing a \
numeric simulator structurally cannot: carry a symbolic parameter (a whole \
code family indexed by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "00d7f70d-8d0e-ae34-30ec-ef5724b8f51e"],
 ", and a continuous error angle ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2f3e8453-9762-68ea-56c5-1e1d22173a30"],
 ") and certify the answer in a single evaluation."
}], "Text",
 CellID->28909615,ExpressionUUID->"5902d484-617f-9570-0f31-cdee100bfc5a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In other words, I have tried to build a small, honest instrument. It does \
not race Stim on scale or speed, because it is not that kind of tool: it \
computes things exactly, and exact minimum distance is NP-hard, so it lives \
at small ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "56e0e12c-de7d-6135-7e9f-f889db08301f"],
 " by mathematical necessity. What it offers instead is exactness and \
symbolic generality, and the whole notebook is arranged so you can watch both \
claims be checked rather than take them on faith. I strongly believe in a \
computation-first narrative for learning: in a sense, if I cannot compute it, \
I cannot claim to understand it."
}], "Text",
 CellID->193832008,ExpressionUUID->"5708d032-2150-04f2-7d37-ca7c50e56ed8"],

Cell["\<\
Before we start, a few notes on the environment. This is a live Wolfram \
notebook: evaluate the cells from top to bottom. Some cells define objects \
(the code list, a handful of small symplectic functions, a Python session) \
that later cells use, so order matters. The narrative runs as one continuous \
sequence, like a movie, with headings only as gentle transitions. My \
suggestion is to read each output and its meaning first, and only then unpack \
the input code that produced it.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->684317082,ExpressionUUID->"3b7807e8-9504-b25a-6907-9eb21cb600a0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Remember that you are not locked into the code as given. You can (and \
should) change the codes, feed in your own stabilizer generators, push the \
symbolic family further, and re-run. That is the point of shipping this as a \
notebook rather than a PDF. If you have questions, reach out at ",
 ButtonBox["quantum@wolfram.com",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["mailto:quantum@wolfram.com"], None}],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellID->528485616,ExpressionUUID->"166f8969-5e31-e8e5-3e14-21335bc5bacf"],

Cell["Prerequisites and How to Read This", "Subsection",
 CellID->1392207227,ExpressionUUID->"234f1fb7-07c6-bba9-5174-96f1b27009f3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "You should be comfortable with the stabilizer formalism at the level of a \
first course: Pauli operators, stabilizer generators, the codespace as their \
joint +1 eigenspace, and logical operators as the normalizer modulo the \
stabilizer group. The binary symplectic picture (each Pauli as a vector over \
GF(2), commutation as a symplectic inner product) is built up from scratch in \
Part II, so no prior fluency there is required. The cross-check in Part III \
calls Stim through ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  ButtonBox["ExternalEvaluate",
   BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
   ButtonData->{
     URL["https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/ExternalEvaluate.html"], 
     None}]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "6ee45b93-a0df-d1f6-169c-52dcd16ba3dc"],
 "; if you do not have Stim installed those cells will say so and you can \
skip them, keeping the independent re-derivation as your check."
}], "Text",
 CellID->705466992,ExpressionUUID->"0a3edd72-e0d3-62fb-3bc5-d53f916630c1"],

Cell["Let's start!", "Text",
 CellID->1350516518,ExpressionUUID->"6ec9a758-58be-624c-16f6-3f02594b3096"],

Cell["Part I: The Open Niche Beside a Fast Simulator", "Section",
 CellID->907546795,ExpressionUUID->"23253b8d-1c99-a6bf-52da-b2d7952d0c85"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A stabilizer code on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6e84829b-9667-6594-16bb-1bc523124ffe"],
 " physical qubits is defined by a set of commuting Pauli operators, its \
stabilizer generators. The codespace is their simultaneous +1 eigenspace, and \
if there are ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["m", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "569e173f-6b36-b240-7ea5-8f790ec000aa"],
 " independent generators the code stores ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["k", "TI"], "=", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], "-", 
   StyleBox["m", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "15a3e88e-0b57-fa6e-4350-01e8aee3c8b4"],
 " logical qubits. An error is ",
 StyleBox["detectable", "TI"],
 " when it anticommutes with at least one generator (it flips that \
generator's measured eigenvalue, a syndrome bit), and ",
 StyleBox["undetectable", "TI"],
 " when it commutes with all of them. The dangerous errors are the \
undetectable ones that still act nontrivially on the codespace: these are the \
logical operators, and the code distance is the weight of the lightest one."
}], "Text",
 CellID->814772321,ExpressionUUID->"779c57de-bb93-d239-1da4-4e983e27a103"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Equivalently, and this is the representation the whole subject runs on, \
each Pauli string on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2867ea9b-6e8d-517b-581c-03c50b382041"],
 " qubits becomes a binary vector of length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"2", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "76dfbc62-92ac-7b6e-1ee4-364c275949b4"],
 ": an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0df4b6a5-173c-e15a-3b8b-2f0fa2ceb3a0"],
 " contributes to the first half, a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4d620fc7-5cf2-d9d8-7b11-a6a15484ab22"],
 " to the second, a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Y", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "741b091d-feb2-1a39-1c38-a146fb476903"],
 " to both. Two Paulis commute exactly when a bilinear form over GF(2), the \
symplectic product ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\[Omega]", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3bf5a20e-c541-e06e-698a-446becf5b2b4"],
 ", vanishes between their vectors. In short, the entire question \"is this \
error detectable, is it logical, how heavy is the lightest logical\" becomes \
linear algebra over the two-element field."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1054628905,ExpressionUUID->"07964fa7-8d1e-ff14-2dae-660124a8c27e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Why is this worth a dedicated exact tool when Stim already simulates \
stabilizer circuits at enormous scale? Because the two live on different \
axes. Stim samples: it runs stabilizer circuits and detector-error models \
blindingly fast, over thousands of qubits, which is exactly what decoder \
benchmarking and threshold estimation need. It does not hand you the exact \
minimum distance of a code, and for a deep reason. Computing the exact \
minimum distance of a stabilizer code is NP-hard (Kapshikar and Kundu, ",
 ButtonBox["arXiv:2203.04262",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.04262"], None}],
 "), so any tool that returns the ",
 StyleBox["exact", "TI"],
 " answer is confined to small codes no matter how it is written. That is not \
a limitation to apologize for; it is the shape of the problem. The textbook \
codes are small, and small is precisely where an exact, symbolic treatment \
earns its keep."
}], "Text",
 CellID->2043779557,ExpressionUUID->"3e367b18-9aa8-58f9-67ce-73421f5d2ac3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The other axis is symbolic generality. A numeric simulator represents one \
concrete code at a time: fixed ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1dbaaaf7-1388-e538-4b59-43b1a622cc02"],
 ", fixed generators, fixed angles. A symbolic engine can carry ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "77d99fdb-a351-2d53-1de6-1684d6e477b9"],
 " as a symbol and certify a statement about an entire infinite family in one \
evaluation, or carry an error angle ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "04646437-faee-7dfc-2c1c-7e70fe9b47c6"],
 " and return the syndrome expectation as an exact function of it. Neither of \
those is a thing Stim is built to do, and neither competes with what Stim is \
built to do. The tools are complementary. This notebook stakes out the \
exact-and-symbolic corner and checks, at every step, that its exact answers \
agree with Stim's wherever the two overlap."
}], "Text",
 CellID->17830494,ExpressionUUID->"1cbde497-e826-5557-4442-fdd089d33fbd"],

Cell["\<\
The exact code-analysis layer used here ships in the QuantumFramework paclet, \
following the constructions in Gottesman's thesis. Let us load it: register \
the paclet directory, then read in the QEC package. The paths below point to \
a local QuantumFramework checkout; adjust them to your own.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1589986527,ExpressionUUID->"6ecb37d0-fbcc-3e82-16c8-ae9dfe7e84e8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{"PacletDirectoryLoad", 
  "[", "\"\</Users/mohammadb/Documents/GitHub/QuantumFramework/\
QuantumFramework/\>\"", "]"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<Wolfram`QuantumFramework`\>\"", "]"}], "\n"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"Get", 
  "[", "\"\</Users/mohammadb/Documents/GitHub/QuantumFramework/\
OngoingProjects/QEC/QEC/QEC.wl\>\"", "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[1]:= ",
 CellID->428691732,ExpressionUUID->"4c358d3e-86d3-ec5f-75ca-25783367b6a5"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"/Users/mohammadb/Documents/GitHub/MarkdownToNotebook/examples/\
Paclet/WolframParser\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"/Users/mohammadb/Documents/GitHub/QuantumFramework/\
QuantumFramework/\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[1]= ",
 CellID->1641250213,ExpressionUUID->"1f908cf3-3c70-d55a-45af-25bdb50abfa0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
A code is an object you can ask questions of. Build the Steane code and read \
its parameters and generators:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->67888809,ExpressionUUID->"5dac66ba-272d-3627-0b54-7fe452d99d0d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"steane", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"QECCode", "[", "\"\<SteaneCode\>\"", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"steane", "[", "\"\<Parameters\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"steane", "[", "\"\<Generators\>\"", "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[2]:= ",
 CellID->1801257020,ExpressionUUID->"110914de-bbdd-83a1-4736-8d983e69d18b"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"7", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"\<\"IIIXXXX\"\>", ",", "\<\"XIXIXIX\"\>", ",", "\<\"IXXIIXX\"\>",
      ",", "\<\"IIIZZZZ\"\>", ",", "\<\"ZIZIZIZ\"\>", 
     ",", "\<\"IZZIIZZ\"\>"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[2]= ",
 CellID->358626973,ExpressionUUID->"76d004f9-29e5-6665-1eef-9da34f904d4f"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first entry is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["k", "TI"], "}"}]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2c76c797-c249-79ca-540d-ded0f7fc4b10"],
 ", the physical and logical qubit counts; the second is the six Pauli-string \
generators, three all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "35077f55-9291-6519-633d-771f27244c63"],
 " checks and three all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4ebf3687-f906-130d-7644-afe0f8b37e57"],
 " checks, the hallmark of a CSS code."
}], "Text",
 CellID->876231958,ExpressionUUID->"65de4a87-3471-cd9f-13e6-63e1bd0597e5"],

Cell["Part II: The Five Textbook Codes, Exactly", "Section",
 CellID->1020575516,ExpressionUUID->"7bedd9bd-1d84-ddaa-2992-d0e79436a7f0"],

Cell["\<\
Collect the five codes we will study into one association, so later cells can \
sweep over all of them at once:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1967685850,ExpressionUUID->"4c37f35a-e1a6-07fe-75cc-eb0790536dc4"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"codes", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"AssociationMap", "[", "\n", "    ", 
    RowBox[{"QECCode", ",", "\n", "    ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"\"\<BitFlipCode\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<PhaseFlipCode\>\"", ",", 
       " ", "\"\<ShorCode\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<5QubitCode\>\"", ",", 
       " ", "\"\<SteaneCode\>\""}], "}"}]}], "\n", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[3]:= ",
 CellID->1728662077,ExpressionUUID->"02f88238-86c8-66df-3287-2462337d4c25"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", ",", "\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"ShorCode\"\>", ",", "\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"SteaneCode\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[3]= ",
 CellID->1356957898,ExpressionUUID->"034d641c-5834-e412-3173-7e4659c6cf78"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["The Symplectic Picture: A Pauli as a Vector over GF(2)", "Subsection",
 CellID->1653200050,ExpressionUUID->"52ce4f6a-032d-a48b-02f6-6734b6d90ed1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To verify the package's answers independently, we will not call the \
package's own distance or logical-operator routines and ask them to check \
themselves. Instead we rebuild the underlying linear algebra from scratch, in \
a handful of tiny functions, and let ",
 StyleBox["those", "TI"],
 " pass judgment. This is also the cleanest way to see the machinery turn."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1384978530,ExpressionUUID->"5bfe104a-50de-a555-0985-8a1461680fbf"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Start at the bottom of the ladder: map a Pauli string to its binary \
symplectic vector, an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "134534fb-1061-d656-417a-ada5a115bcbc"],
 "-block followed by a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2cd98fd2-34e7-e188-54e6-269cbc93b3d2"],
 "-block. Define the per-letter rule and assemble:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->519689844,ExpressionUUID->"6e1c99a5-dcef-9c28-1623-100ed49be772"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"xzOf", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"<|", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\"\<I\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}]}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<X\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}]}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<Y\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}]}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<Z\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}]}]}], "|>"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"strip", "[", "str_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"StringDelete", "[", 
    RowBox[{"str", ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"StartOfString", " ", "~~", " ", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<+\>\"", " ", "|", " ", "\"\<-\>\""}], ")"}]}]}], "]"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "str_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"ps", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"xzOf", " ", "/@", " ", 
        RowBox[{"Characters", "[", 
         RowBox[{"strip", "[", "str", "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
     "    ", 
     RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"ps", "[", 
        RowBox[{"[", 
         RowBox[{"All", ",", " ", "1"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"ps", "[", 
        RowBox[{"[", 
         RowBox[{"All", ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "\n", 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<XZZXI\>\"", "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[4]:= ",
 CellID->558143646,ExpressionUUID->"608e87cc-8fce-afdf-10b5-1e962a78f525"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "1", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "1", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "1", ",", "1", ",",
    "0", ",", "0"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[4]= ",
 CellID->252167569,ExpressionUUID->"2aa912c6-4938-1bb7-5a56-8ba068cd699d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first five entries are the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "66b4bee0-d247-b105-0e4b-37cde7f3006f"],
 "-pattern, the last five the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "337c2d89-644e-5b5e-6103-c4d30dfb29a4"],
 "-pattern; an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5f7c2741-1c81-ec9b-0503-bf2b9535b6e1"],
 " shows up in the left half, a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "644cc522-c7c4-9b18-0c73-dc8ff276e662"],
 " in the right, and a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Y", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2de8c8f9-eaf3-8e90-5b97-e1a28e87d4fa"],
 " would show up in both."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1541450781,ExpressionUUID->"6801f052-da94-0b6f-183e-ea1fdf2159b5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Two Paulis commute exactly when the symplectic product of their vectors, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Omega]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["u", "TI"], ",", 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"]}], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "=", 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["u", "TI"]], "\[CenterDot]", 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["z", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"]], "+", 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["z", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["u", "TI"]], "\[CenterDot]", 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"]]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0daf8e98-7628-e40b-3b54-27c203c2cf51"],
 " taken mod 2, is zero. Define it:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1657269721,ExpressionUUID->"7b6211f5-475c-1194-2911-88bf72e963fe"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
    RowBox[{"u_", ",", " ", "v_"}], "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"n", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Length", "[", "u", "]"}], "/", "2"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
     "    ", 
     RowBox[{"Mod", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Take", "[", 
          RowBox[{"u", ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}], " ", ".", " ", 
         RowBox[{"Drop", "[", 
          RowBox[{"v", ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}]}], " ", "+", " ", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Drop", "[", 
          RowBox[{"u", ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}], " ", ".", " ", 
         RowBox[{"Take", "[", 
          RowBox[{"v", ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}]}]}], ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}]}], 
    "\n", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<XII\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<ZII\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<XII\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<IZI\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[5]:= ",
 CellID->2048061839,ExpressionUUID->"7c8f7bdf-2493-8d8a-24b5-72994d27d7d0"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1", ",", "0"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[5]= ",
 CellID->1680809496,ExpressionUUID->"14e8dcaf-74c9-37ba-3c97-d5097d7d9d80"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "As one can see, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["X", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5d5cbd3a-e88d-ee42-0b63-35678939b4a8"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3b7fe4cc-5889-7f4e-6904-fd96593c4594"],
 " anticommute (they share a qubit and disagree there), while ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["X", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6cb5c01f-62f5-68e0-144a-da5916803aca"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "2"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "55f417ec-7166-d78d-038b-8eb60010bdd7"],
 " commute (they act on different qubits). This single function is the whole \
notion of \"detectable\": the syndrome of an error is just its symplectic \
product against each generator."
}], "Text",
 CellID->496109794,ExpressionUUID->"4b6e5dad-9702-0c1f-7916-54f722b75765"],

Cell["\<\
We will also need the weight of a Pauli (how many qubits it touches) and, for \
the distance search, every Pauli of a given weight. Define both:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->108435171,ExpressionUUID->"1d359769-845f-cd94-44ca-0f332deb97fe"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"wt", "[", "str_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Count", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Characters", "[", 
      RowBox[{"strip", "[", "str", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"Except", "[", "\"\<I\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"weightK", "[", 
    RowBox[{"n_", ",", " ", "k_"}], "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"If", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"k", " ", "==", " ", "0"}], ",", "\n", "    ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"StringRepeat", "[", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<I\>\"", ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}], "}"}], ",", "\n", "    ", 
     
     RowBox[{"Catenate", " ", "@", " ", 
      RowBox[{"Map", "[", "\n", "        ", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Function", "[", 
         RowBox[{"pos", ",", "\n", "            ", 
          RowBox[{"StringJoin", " ", "/@", " ", 
           RowBox[{"(", "\n", "                ", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"ReplacePart", "[", 
               RowBox[{
                RowBox[{"ConstantArray", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"\"\<I\>\"", ",", " ", "n"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
                RowBox[{"Thread", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"pos", " ", "->", " ", "#"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], " ", 
              "&"}], " ", "/@", " ", 
             RowBox[{"Tuples", "[", 
              RowBox[{
               RowBox[{"{", 
                
                RowBox[{"\"\<X\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<Y\>\"", ",", 
                 " ", "\"\<Z\>\""}], "}"}], ",", " ", "k"}], "]"}]}], "\n", 
            "            ", ")"}]}]}], "\n", "        ", "]"}], ",", "\n", 
        "        ", 
        RowBox[{"Subsets", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Range", "[", "n", "]"}], ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"{", "k", "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "\n", "    ", "]"}]}]}], "\n", 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Length", "[", 
    RowBox[{"weightK", "[", 
     RowBox[{"5", ",", " ", "1"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Length", "[", 
    RowBox[{"weightK", "[", 
     RowBox[{"5", ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[6]:= ",
 CellID->525840340,ExpressionUUID->"2055bfee-d4b5-7aa2-506a-36cbdd404888"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"15", ",", "90"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[6]= ",
 CellID->1708324045,ExpressionUUID->"64c30c0d-1857-1b13-0cf0-a65799e26679"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On five qubits there are ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"3", "\[CenterDot]", "5"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "46632a09-cc5f-b0ef-6e10-cc52e5eb0235"],
 " single-qubit Paulis and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SuperscriptBox["3", "2"], 
   TemplateBox[{"5", "2"},
    "Binomial"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6ea251ed-865f-e03c-1652-48b733ebb306"],
 " weight-two Paulis; the counts confirm the enumerator is complete, which \
matters because the distance search below trusts it to miss nothing."
}], "Text",
 CellID->203008389,ExpressionUUID->"25a0bfbe-b1d4-2428-535f-36fbc0668f72"],

Cell["Distance from First Principles", "Subsection",
 CellID->1090263105,ExpressionUUID->"04786d0e-016a-d127-2c07-8568b01ca00d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here is the definition made computational. A Pauli is a logical operator \
when it lies in the normalizer but not in the stabilizer group: it commutes \
with every generator (syndrome zero) ",
 StyleBox["and", "TI"],
 " it is not itself a product of generators. The second condition is the one \
that is easy to get backwards. Membership in the stabilizer group is a GF(2) \
span question, and the correct test is that appending the candidate's vector \
to the generator matrix leaves the rank unchanged; so \"not in the group\" \
means the rank strictly ",
 StyleBox["rises", "TI"],
 ". Define the logical test with the rank-rises condition spelled out:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1560876240,ExpressionUUID->"2d15b43a-cd49-5244-5b2a-2e67e4fc20ae"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"genMatrix", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"symOf", " ", "/@", " ", 
    RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Generators\>\"", "]"}]}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"logicalVecQ", "[", 
    RowBox[{"code_", ",", " ", "v_"}], "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", "\n", "    ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"sg", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"genMatrix", "[", "code", "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"m", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<StabilizerCount\>\"", "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",",
      "\n", "    ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
       RowBox[{"sg", ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
           RowBox[{"v", ",", " ", "#"}], "]"}], " ", "===", " ", "0"}], " ", 
         "&"}]}], "]"}], " ", "&&", " ", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"MatrixRank", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Append", "[", 
          RowBox[{"sg", ",", " ", "v"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
         RowBox[{"Modulus", " ", "->", " ", "2"}]}], "]"}], " ", ">", " ", 
       "m"}]}]}], "\n", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"logicalVecQ", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "\"\<BitFlipCode\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<ZII\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", "\n", " ", 
   RowBox[{"logicalVecQ", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "\"\<BitFlipCode\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<ZZI\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[7]:= ",
 CellID->201914069,ExpressionUUID->"2d1ff276-b82a-c3ca-5b24-f43039dc9110"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", "False"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[7]= ",
 CellID->607076188,ExpressionUUID->"14fd1512-f616-8834-3c82-8d5f83a0db1e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "As expected, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "66c67103-72a1-9427-0efe-696d0755ff0d"],
 " passes both tests for the bit-flip code (undetectable and outside the \
group, so a genuine logical), while the generator ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"], 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "2"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "48c794f4-84f6-2bfc-78fc-0dbe2c8379c6"],
 " fails the second test: it commutes with everything, but it ",
 StyleBox["is", "TI"],
 " in the group, so its rank does not rise. We will come back to what that \
first result means for the bit-flip code, because it is the honest surprise \
of this whole subject."
}], "Text",
 CellID->807053435,ExpressionUUID->"58ee7ecb-1370-1005-0896-7795a605636f"],

Cell["\<\
The distance is now a search: the smallest weight at which a logical exists, \
and a witness achieving it. Climb the weights and stop at the first hit:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1633675818,ExpressionUUID->"11d04a92-cbef-a8a5-47e0-63dfed9bfa8f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"firstLogicalOfWeight", "[", 
    RowBox[{"code_", ",", " ", "w_"}], "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"SelectFirst", "[", "\n", "    ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"weightK", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Qubits\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", "w"}], "]"}], ",",
      " ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"logicalVecQ", "[", 
       RowBox[{"code", ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "#", "]"}]}], "]"}], " ", "&"}]}], "\n", 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"independentWitness", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", "w", "}"}], ",", "\n", "    ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"w", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"SelectFirst", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Range", "[", 
          RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Qubits\>\"", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"!", " ", 
           RowBox[{"MissingQ", "[", 
            RowBox[{"firstLogicalOfWeight", "[", 
             RowBox[{"code", ",", " ", "#"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], " ", "&"}]}], 
        "]"}]}], ";", "\n", "    ", 
      RowBox[{"If", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"MissingQ", "[", "w", "]"}], ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"Missing", "[", "\"\<NoLogical\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"firstLogicalOfWeight", "[", 
         RowBox[{"code", ",", " ", "w"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}]}], "\n", "]"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"independentDistance", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"x", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"independentWitness", "[", "code", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
     "    ", 
     RowBox[{"If", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"MissingQ", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", " ", "Infinity", ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"wt", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "\n", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"independentWitness", "[", 
  RowBox[{"codes", "[", "\"\<5QubitCode\>\"", "]"}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[8]:= ",
 CellID->817696651,ExpressionUUID->"7d9821c9-0e61-bc6d-2ba7-b893ab1506b7"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"XYXII\"\>"], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[8]= ",
 CellID->1663470633,ExpressionUUID->"3181a460-bc62-f1ee-67be-3e4a3514c334"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
This witness is a lightest logical for the five-qubit code, found by our own \
search with no reference to the package. Its weight is the code distance.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1232986364,ExpressionUUID->"3937e1d4-04c3-e55a-68cc-f89ead77cfa0"],

Cell["\<\
Now put the package and the independent re-derivation side by side. For each \
code we show its parameters as the code itself reports them, the package's \
minimum-weight logical, our independently computed distance, and whether the \
two distances agree:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->762269937,ExpressionUUID->"6b45e796-37a7-eecc-197a-fed04253b416"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Grid", "[", "\n", "    ", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Prepend", "[", "\n", "        ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Function", "[", 
       RowBox[{"name", ",", "\n", "            ", 
        RowBox[{"With", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"c", " ", "=", " ", 
             RowBox[{"codes", "[", "name", "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
            RowBox[{"di", " ", "=", " ", 
             RowBox[{"independentDistance", "[", 
              RowBox[{"codes", "[", "name", "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", 
          "\n", "                ", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"name", ",", "\n", "                 ", 
            RowBox[{"StringJoin", "[", 
             RowBox[{"\"\<[[\>\"", ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{"ToString", "[", 
               RowBox[{"c", "[", "\"\<Qubits\>\"", "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
              " ", "\"\<,\>\"", ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{"ToString", "[", 
               RowBox[{"c", "[", "\"\<LogicalQubits\>\"", "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
              " ", "\"\<,\>\"", ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{"ToString", "[", 
               RowBox[{"CodeDistance", "[", "c", "]"}], "]"}], ",", 
              " ", "\"\<]]\>\""}], "]"}], ",", "\n", "                 ", 
            RowBox[{"MinimumWeightLogical", "[", "c", "]"}], ",", " ", "di", ",",
             " ", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{"CodeDistance", "[", "c", "]"}], " ", "===", " ", 
             "di"}]}], "}"}]}], "\n", "            ", "]"}]}], "\n", 
       "        ", "]"}], " ", "/@", " ", 
      RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], ",", "\n", "        ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"Style", "[", 
        RowBox[{"#", ",", " ", "Bold"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], " ", "/@", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"\"\<code\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<[[n,k,d]]\>\"", ",", 
        " ", "\"\<package witness\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<independent d\>\"", ",",
         " ", "\"\<agree\>\""}], "}"}]}]}], "\n", "    ", "]"}], ",", "\n", 
   "    ", 
   RowBox[{"Frame", " ", "->", " ", "All"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Alignment", " ", "->", " ", "Left"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Spacings", " ", "->", " ", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"1.5", ",", " ", "0.8"}], "}"}]}]}], "\n", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[9]:= ",
 CellID->1596700196,ExpressionUUID->"5e187950-8ca2-2ab2-0638-711a25577898"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"code\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\<\"[[n,k,d]]\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\<\"package witness\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\<\"independent d\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold], 
     StyleBox["\<\"agree\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontWeight->Bold]},
    {"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", "\<\"[[3,1,1]]\"\>", "\<\"ZII\"\>", "1", "True"},
    {"\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", "\<\"[[3,1,1]]\"\>", "\<\"XII\"\>", "1", "True"},
    {"\<\"ShorCode\"\>", "\<\"[[9,1,3]]\"\>", "\<\"XXXIIIIII\"\>", "3", 
     "True"},
    {"\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", "\<\"[[5,1,3]]\"\>", "\<\"XYXII\"\>", "3", 
     "True"},
    {"\<\"SteaneCode\"\>", "\<\"[[7,1,3]]\"\>", "\<\"XXXIIII\"\>", "3", "True"}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{1.5}}, "Rows" -> {{0.8}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[9]= ",
 CellID->1663427655,ExpressionUUID->"250a2d7c-9b81-05b0-5309-c5261e346f9a"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The final column is the whole point of this part: for every code, the \
distance our from-scratch symplectic search returns is exactly the distance \
the package returns. The Shor, five-qubit, and Steane codes come out as the \
familiar distance-three codes; the two three-qubit codes come out at distance \
one, which brings us to the honest surprise.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->2118931930,ExpressionUUID->"15a9104d-5625-6af3-4356-8a1763d038d9"],

Cell["\<\
The Honest Surprise: A Repetition Code Has Quantum Distance One\
\>", "Subsection",
 CellID->1039057480,ExpressionUUID->"58c17d8b-1b7e-6f79-08ff-74d59e0b3243"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The three-qubit bit-flip code is often introduced as a distance-three code, \
and as a ",
 StyleBox["classical", "TI"],
 " code against bit flips it is. As a ",
 StyleBox["quantum", "TI"],
 " code it is not. Look at the syndrome of a single ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "43821c38-6720-b4f9-71b1-966212da1ec3"],
 " on the first qubit, and ask whether that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "341c54d9-3079-6bba-5c24-4d83410c3980"],
 " is a logical operator:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1411198270,ExpressionUUID->"1dbf9c2b-d84a-d79b-4b44-1674d9fcbde1"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"Syndrome", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "\"\<BitFlipCode\>\"", "]"}], ",", 
     " ", "\"\<ZII\>\""}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"logicalVecQ", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "\"\<BitFlipCode\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"symOf", "[", "\"\<ZII\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[10]:= ",
 CellID->1062924604,ExpressionUUID->"0f449897-1a0e-51e7-357b-11d19fbb23cd"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"0", ",", "0"}], "}"}], ",", "True"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[10]= ",
 CellID->827610706,ExpressionUUID->"6d5a63ab-93b9-14f3-1b7a-7af5264c7ed9"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The syndrome is all zeros, so a lone ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5077c06e-c394-b43c-000c-da4bf6261f06"],
 " is undetectable: it commutes with both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "07359582-e823-44a1-2eca-0cef89d62e8b"],
 "-type stabilizers. Yet it is not in the stabilizer group, so it acts \
nontrivially on the codespace. It is therefore a weight-one logical, and the \
quantum code distance is one, not three. In words, protecting against bit \
flips with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7c239bd0-3ff5-3b18-25d0-129ab9818662"],
 "-type checks leaves phase errors completely unguarded, and a single \
unguarded phase error is a logical fault. This is not a defect in the \
computation; it is the reason a real code has to protect against both error \
types, which is exactly what the Shor code does by nesting the two repetition \
ideas. The package agrees with the mathematics, not with the folklore, and \
that is what we want from it."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1477609488,ExpressionUUID->"68c76573-72ce-0c1a-18fd-7d3d077b5760"],

Cell["Logical Operators and Their Algebra", "Subsection",
 CellID->1030824397,ExpressionUUID->"6184d2f2-049e-728e-17bb-cbbcad2b40d4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A code with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["k", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4fd967ef-5008-0b29-75e7-7ec961bd5673"],
 " logical qubits comes with logical operators ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    StyleBox["X", "TI"], "_"], 
   StyleBox["i", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "19bb1c5e-57ab-6e36-4f58-9618625e351c"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "_"], 
   StyleBox["i", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5c8dcc56-7aa9-964a-04b2-e6107f5dfc90"],
 ", and they must obey the same algebra as bare Paulis on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["k", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "30baa1c8-66ad-75e0-6059-389213585fca"],
 " qubits: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    StyleBox["X", "TI"], "_"], 
   StyleBox["i", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "52535901-cb26-8b9c-0261-516aeed0d9e6"],
 " anticommutes with its partner ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "_"], 
   StyleBox["i", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "323a4b1c-b138-a791-63da-6359c0b20dfb"],
 ", commutes with every other logical, all the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  OverscriptBox[
   StyleBox["X", "TI"], "_"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7421cf44-a1ac-4319-1dde-e721d0592e63"],
 " commute among themselves, all the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  OverscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "_"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "511b3347-8d7d-ab60-0738-ab212409f64a"],
 " commute among themselves, and every logical commutes with every stabilizer \
while lying outside the group. The package produces logical operators from a \
standard-form reduction; we check the entire algebra independently with our \
symplectic product and rank test:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->100031176,ExpressionUUID->"36c19c85-764d-0b01-5efe-15ef03f8566b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"logicalAlgebraOkQ", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Module", "[", "\n", "    ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"logs", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"LogicalOperators", "[", "code", "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"sg", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"genMatrix", "[", "code", "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"m", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<StabilizerCount\>\"", "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
       "xs", ",", " ", "zs", ",", " ", "k"}], "}"}], ",", "\n", "    ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"xs", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"symOf", " ", "/@", " ", 
        RowBox[{"logs", "[", "\"\<X\>\"", "]"}]}]}], ";", " ", 
      RowBox[{"zs", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"symOf", " ", "/@", " ", 
        RowBox[{"logs", "[", "\"\<Z\>\"", "]"}]}]}], ";", " ", 
      RowBox[{"k", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"Length", "[", "xs", "]"}]}], ";", "\n", "    ", 
      RowBox[{"And", "[", "\n", "        ", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Tuples", "[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"Range", "[", "k", "]"}], ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], ",", 
          "\n", "            ", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"With", "[", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{"{", 
              RowBox[{
               RowBox[{"i", " ", "=", " ", 
                RowBox[{"#", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
               RowBox[{"j", " ", "=", " ", 
                RowBox[{"#", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "2", "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
             RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
               RowBox[{
                RowBox[{"xs", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "i", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
                RowBox[{"zs", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "j", "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], " ", "===", " ", 
              RowBox[{"If", "[", 
               RowBox[{
                RowBox[{"i", " ", "===", " ", "j"}], ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", 
                "0"}], "]"}]}]}], "]"}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}], ",", "\n", 
        "        ", 
        RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Subsets", "[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"Range", "[", "k", "]"}], ",", " ", 
            RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
             RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"xs", "[", 
               RowBox[{"[", 
                RowBox[{"#", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{"xs", "[", 
               RowBox[{"[", 
                RowBox[{"#", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "2", "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], " ", "===",
             " ", "0"}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}], ",", "\n", "        ", 
        RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Subsets", "[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"Range", "[", "k", "]"}], ",", " ", 
            RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
             RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"zs", "[", 
               RowBox[{"[", 
                RowBox[{"#", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{"zs", "[", 
               RowBox[{"[", 
                RowBox[{"#", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"[", "2", "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], " ", "===",
             " ", "0"}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}], ",", "\n", "        ", 
        RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
           RowBox[{"xs", ",", " ", "zs"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"Function", "[", 
           RowBox[{"l", ",", " ", 
            RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
             RowBox[{"sg", ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{
               RowBox[{
                RowBox[{"omega", "[", 
                 RowBox[{"l", ",", " ", "#"}], "]"}], " ", "===", " ", "0"}], 
               " ", "&"}]}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", "\n", "        ", 
        RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
           RowBox[{"xs", ",", " ", "zs"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"MatrixRank", "[", 
             RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"Append", "[", 
               RowBox[{"sg", ",", " ", "#"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
              RowBox[{"Modulus", " ", "->", " ", "2"}]}], "]"}], " ", ">", 
            " ", "m"}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}]}], "\n", "    ", "]"}]}]}], "\n", 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"AssociationMap", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"logicalAlgebraOkQ", "[", 
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "#", "]"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[11]:= ",
 CellID->384354388,ExpressionUUID->"6815cd54-0cd7-196f-182a-e51ea5626bb5"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"ShorCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"SteaneCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[11]= ",
 CellID->1325609351,ExpressionUUID->"519775d4-d545-25f2-07ad-6c91dcf18fd8"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Every code's logical operators pass the full algebra check. Note that a \
minimum-weight witness from the distance search need not be one of these \
canonical ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  OverscriptBox[
   StyleBox["X", "TI"], "_"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5900aac7-5263-773e-0f3f-43a167165a04"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  OverscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "_"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "06ecfdb3-2197-c9b0-2e93-f4fd50239b9a"],
 ": the standard-form logicals are a valid ",
 StyleBox["generating", "TI"],
 " pair, while the witness is the lightest element of the whole coset, and \
the two answer different questions."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1443527617,ExpressionUUID->"12d95fcb-3316-2212-42e7-569546a37178"],

Cell["A Sign-Exact Encoder", "Subsection",
 CellID->311511756,ExpressionUUID->"733995c2-f5bf-78ee-22c6-0caffc4a4a34"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The last exact object is the encoder: a Clifford circuit that prepares a \
codeword from the all-zeros state. \"Prepares a codeword\" has to mean the +1 \
eigenstate of every generator, with the sign exactly +1 and not ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4f8ab258-7e7f-0723-7589-34027b0a6a09"],
 ", because a stabilizer with the wrong sign describes a different state \
entirely. We check this at the level of the state vector, comparing vectors \
rather than fidelities, so a sign error cannot hide."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1381425643,ExpressionUUID->"6b12ee74-9953-131d-1921-003e18e67e67"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Build the Pauli operators as honest matrices, run the real encoding circuit \
on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    RowBox[{"0", "\[Ellipsis]", "0"}]},
   "Ket"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "65262d3c-fccc-1dc6-0cdd-c579f579672c"],
 ", and confirm each generator leaves the resulting vector fixed. The zero \
test is symbolic, so it certifies exact equality rather than numerical \
closeness:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->377280352,ExpressionUUID->"5e157ee2-48da-d240-062b-77cc696ca0aa"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"pmat", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"<|", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\"\<I\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<X\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", "\n", 
     "         ", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<Y\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"-", "I"}]}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"I", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<Z\>\"", " ", "->", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}], "|>"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"pauliOp", "[", "str_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Fold", "[", 
    RowBox[{"KroneckerProduct", ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"pmat", " ", "/@", " ", 
      RowBox[{"Characters", "[", 
       RowBox[{"strip", "[", "str", "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"encodedVector", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Normal", "[", "\n", "    ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"EncodingCircuit", "[", "code", "]"}], "[", "\n", "        ", 
      RowBox[{"QuantumState", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"SparseArray", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"1", " ", "->", " ", "1"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"2", "^", 
           RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Qubits\>\"", "]"}]}]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
        
        RowBox[{"ConstantArray", "[", 
         RowBox[{"2", ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Qubits\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "]"}], 
      "\n", "    ", "]"}], "[", "\"\<StateVector\>\"", "]"}], "\n", "]"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"encoderFixesQ", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"psi", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"encodedVector", "[", "code", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
     "    ", 
     RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Generators\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"pauliOp", "[", "#", "]"}], " ", ".", " ", "psi"}], " ", "-",
            " ", "psi"}], ",", " ", "PossibleZeroQ"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}]}], 
      "]"}]}], "\n", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"AssociationMap", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"encoderFixesQ", "[", 
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "#", "]"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[12]:= ",
 CellID->353955539,ExpressionUUID->"78287f93-0f12-7c52-2828-76ddaaa746b8"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"ShorCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"SteaneCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[12]= ",
 CellID->631628885,ExpressionUUID->"08285353-0e0e-39f8-3828-4b1dabb88bc2"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
For each code, applying any generator to the encoded state returns the state \
unchanged, exactly: the encoder prepares a genuine +1 codeword, sign \
included. The Pauli matrices here are built with qubit 1 as the leftmost \
tensor factor, which is the same ordering QuantumFramework uses for its state \
vectors, so the comparison is apples to apples.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1781880651,ExpressionUUID->"1daed7d3-93e8-7698-4b55-ce9d259d5ce2"],

Cell["Part III: Cross-Checking Against Stim", "Section",
 CellID->1536527082,ExpressionUUID->"187adc3d-3961-76c2-4819-d667b8145c28"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The independent re-derivation lives entirely inside the Wolfram Language, \
so it shares the linear-algebra substrate with the package. A second, \
genuinely external opinion is worth having, and Stim is the natural source: \
it is the reference stabilizer simulator, written in C++, with an independent \
tableau engine. We open a Python session and confirm Stim is importable; if \
this cell reports that Stim is missing, install it with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"pip", " ", "install", " ", "stim"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "641c78eb-7f03-7846-0c24-71b57ab64aac"],
 " into the Python that ",
 Cell[BoxData["ExternalEvaluate"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5bfcc75d-c21d-6fb0-0983-df06f7a8359a"],
 " uses, or skip Part III and rely on the re-derivation above."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1621535814,ExpressionUUID->"112ae6ec-4b5c-6ad2-4729-ffb66ee93838"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"stimSession", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"StartExternalSession", "[", 
    RowBox[{"<|", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<System\>\"", " ", "->", " ", "\"\<Python\>\""}], "|>"}], 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"stimVersion", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Check", "[", "\n", "    ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"ExternalEvaluate", "[", 
     RowBox[{
     "stimSession", ",", " ", "\"\<import stim; stim.__version__\>\""}], 
     "]"}], ",", "\n", 
    "    ", "\"\<stim not available: skip Part III or pip install stim\>\""}],
    "\n", "]"}]}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[13]:= ",
 CellID->482293909,ExpressionUUID->"6121b9de-dddd-aca8-10de-309bd469f6f2"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"1.16.0\"\>"], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[13]= ",
 CellID->885243818,ExpressionUUID->"30209679-023a-ee91-61df-0823b7ccb4fb"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["The Converter and the Syndrome Differential", "Subsection",
 CellID->105329933,ExpressionUUID->"29bc1606-c22d-409d-5f43-8863f7d812e7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The converter is small because both sides speak Pauli strings. Stim writes \
identity as an underscore, so the only translation is a character swap; a \
syndrome bit is then Stim's own commutation test between a generator and an \
error. Define the Stim-side syndrome and compare it, for every code, against \
the package's ",
 Cell[BoxData["Syndrome"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7bf1e1bf-5b3e-c64b-298e-f8f95ec8ac91"],
 " across all weight-one and weight-two errors:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1234416869,ExpressionUUID->"3119d855-3ffd-7401-6726-d21fb9885f6b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"stimSyndrome", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"ExternalEvaluate", "[", 
    RowBox[{
    "stimSession", ",", 
     " ", "\"\<\ndef stim_syndrome(gens, err):\n    e = \
stim.PauliString(err.replace('I','_'))\n    return [0 if \
stim.PauliString(g.replace('I','_')).commutes(e) else 1 for g in gens]\n\
stim_syndrome\>\""}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"syndromeMatchesStimQ", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"gens", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Generators\>\"", "]"}]}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"n", " ", "=", " ", 
        RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Qubits\>\"", "]"}]}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
     "    ", 
     RowBox[{"With", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"errs", " ", "=", " ", 
         RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"weightK", "[", 
            RowBox[{"n", ",", " ", "1"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
           RowBox[{"weightK", "[", 
            RowBox[{"n", ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
       "        ", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"Syndrome", "[", 
            RowBox[{"code", ",", " ", "#"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], " ", "/@", 
          " ", "errs"}], ")"}], " ", "===", " ", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"stimSyndrome", "[", 
            RowBox[{"gens", ",", " ", "#"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], " ", "/@", 
          " ", "errs"}], ")"}]}]}], "\n", "    ", "]"}]}], "\n", "]"}]}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"AssociationMap", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"syndromeMatchesStimQ", "[", 
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "#", "]"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[14]:= ",
 CellID->249718593,ExpressionUUID->"62a6d1b5-4e36-fa69-125d-c8ff6bc0c8b3"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"ShorCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"SteaneCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[14]= ",
 CellID->1625568682,ExpressionUUID->"7c41cdc9-5ab3-1ef5-247e-e4935f4664df"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
For every code, the package's syndrome and Stim's syndrome agree on the \
entire weight-one and weight-two error set, hundreds of errors per code. The \
syndrome layer is exactly what a real decoder consumes, so this is the \
differential that matters most for interoperability.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1771870337,ExpressionUUID->"51fce014-dca1-0630-0783-fa51d5546d1a"],

Cell["The Encoder Differential", "Subsection",
 CellID->305160771,ExpressionUUID->"657c1c65-980e-511d-1303-964f19bb2067"],

Cell["\<\
Stim can also judge the encoder, and more stringently than a syndrome check: \
we translate the encoding gates into a Stim circuit, run Stim's tableau \
simulator, and ask Stim for the expectation value of each of our generators \
on the state it prepared. A value of +1 means Stim agrees the prepared state \
is stabilized by that generator with the correct sign:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1896945989,ExpressionUUID->"0f15dbd5-5f35-596c-352a-d29f5ac02bb6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"stimEncoderExp", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"ExternalEvaluate", "[", 
    RowBox[{
    "stimSession", ",", 
     " ", "\"\<\ndef stim_encoder_exp(gates, gens):\n    circ = \
stim.Circuit()\n    tr = {'CNOT':'CX'}\n    for name, args in gates:\n        \
circ.append(tr.get(name, name), [a-1 for a in args])\n    sim = \
stim.TableauSimulator(); sim.do_circuit(circ)\n    return \
[int(sim.peek_observable_expectation(stim.PauliString(g.replace('I','_')))) \
for g in gens]\nstim_encoder_exp\>\""}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"gatePairs", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"#", "[", 
        RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"Flatten", "[", 
        RowBox[{"{", 
         RowBox[{"#", "[", 
          RowBox[{"[", "2", "]"}], "]"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], " ", "&"}], 
    " ", "/@", " ", 
    RowBox[{"EncodingGates", "[", "code", "]"}]}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"stimEncoderOkQ", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"AllTrue", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"stimEncoderExp", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"gatePairs", "[", "code", "]"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Generators\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"#", " ", "===", " ", "1"}], " ", "&"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"AssociationMap", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"stimEncoderOkQ", "[", 
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "#", "]"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[15]:= ",
 CellID->471728701,ExpressionUUID->"7e9b7e66-629a-1114-26b9-7040172f607e"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"ShorCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"SteaneCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", "True"}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[15]= ",
 CellID->1914386876,ExpressionUUID->"585a51e6-774f-2dd2-087a-48c002fa7738"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Every generator has Stim-expectation +1 on the encoded state, for every code. \
This is the same sign-exact statement we checked with our own Pauli matrices \
in Part II, now confirmed by an independent simulator through a completely \
different route (a tableau update, not a dense state vector).\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1067269536,ExpressionUUID->"1d7c0b5b-c40c-ea6a-4b03-a304edbeb8b0"],

Cell["The Witness Differential", "Subsection",
 CellID->49659130,ExpressionUUID->"370294fd-9e49-2d35-5d31-0da71bfc741f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Finally, Stim can certify the distance witness without ever enumerating \
errors. A minimum-weight logical must be ",
 StyleBox["undetectable", "TI"],
 " (it commutes with every generator) and ",
 StyleBox["nontrivial", "TI"],
 " (it anticommutes with at least one logical operator, so it acts on the \
codespace). Those two facts together put it in the normalizer but outside the \
stabilizer group, which is the definition of a logical. Ask Stim for both:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->464508221,ExpressionUUID->"2845529c-454d-b13b-587b-4bc66cf90001"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"stimWitness", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"ExternalEvaluate", "[", 
    RowBox[{
    "stimSession", ",", 
     " ", "\"\<\ndef stim_witness(gens, logicals, witness):\n    w = \
stim.PauliString(witness.replace('I','_'))\n    undetectable = \
all(stim.PauliString(g.replace('I','_')).commutes(w) for g in gens)\n    \
nontrivial = any(not stim.PauliString(p.replace('I','_')).commutes(w) for p \
in logicals)\n    return [bool(undetectable), bool(nontrivial)]\nstim_witness\
\>\""}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"stimWitnessRes", "[", "code_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"With", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"logs", " ", "=", " ", 
       RowBox[{"LogicalOperators", "[", "code", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\n", 
     "    ", 
     RowBox[{"stimWitness", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"code", "[", "\"\<Generators\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"Join", "[", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"logs", "[", "\"\<X\>\"", "]"}], ",", " ", 
         RowBox[{"logs", "[", "\"\<Z\>\"", "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"MinimumWeightLogical", "[", "code", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "\n", 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"AssociationMap", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"stimWitnessRes", "[", 
     RowBox[{"codes", "[", "#", "]"}], "]"}], " ", "&"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Keys", "[", "codes", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[16]:= ",
 CellID->391084117,ExpressionUUID->"7519012f-e657-215b-2326-988893e273a1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"\<\"BitFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"PhaseFlipCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"ShorCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"5QubitCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"\<\"SteaneCode\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"True", ",", "True"}], "}"}]}]}], 
  "\[RightAssociation]"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[16]= ",
 CellID->625764944,ExpressionUUID->"594776df-9e75-d16a-0f7d-6f991b01a3b0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For each code Stim reports the witness as both undetectable and nontrivial. \
Stim confirms the witness is a valid logical of the stated weight; the ",
 StyleBox["minimality", "TI"],
 " of that weight, that nothing lighter is logical, is what our exhaustive \
symplectic search established, and it is the part no sampler provides. The \
division of labor is the honest one: Stim vouches that the witness is real, \
and the exact search vouches that it is the lightest."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1907680634,ExpressionUUID->"6405505f-4b92-3bd6-0c3b-4a196e24893c"],

Cell["\<\
As a last sanity check on the converter itself, ask Stim for the canonical \
stabilizers of the encoded Steane state and compare them, by eye, with the \
code's own generators:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->483148123,ExpressionUUID->"6878c7f8-419e-9db5-1807-dea27028e79f"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"stimCanonical", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"ExternalEvaluate", "[", 
    RowBox[{
    "stimSession", ",", 
     " ", "\"\<\ndef stim_canonical(gates):\n    circ = stim.Circuit()\n    \
tr = {'CNOT':'CX'}\n    for name, args in gates:\n        \
circ.append(tr.get(name, name), [a-1 for a in args])\n    sim = \
stim.TableauSimulator(); sim.do_circuit(circ)\n    return [str(s) for s in \
sim.canonical_stabilizers()]\nstim_canonical\>\""}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"stimCanonical", "[", 
  RowBox[{"gatePairs", "[", "steane", "]"}], "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[17]:= ",
 CellID->668869836,ExpressionUUID->"48a3d607-c312-9dd3-7850-c860f6a4e739"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"+X_X_X_X\"\>", ",", "\<\"+Z____ZZ\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"+_XX__XX\"\>", ",", "\<\"+_Z__Z_Z\"\>", ",", "\<\"+__Z_ZZ_\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"+___XXXX\"\>", ",", "\<\"+___ZZZZ\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[17]= ",
 CellID->1510519072,ExpressionUUID->"539491e8-328f-8d7b-01ab-08b2473bd441"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Stim returns seven signed stabilizers in its reduced canonical form. These \
generate the same group as our six Steane generators plus the logical ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  OverscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "_"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7fcf59b5-a5ca-2155-25f5-50fecc7f73bf"],
 " that pins the prepared codeword; the +1 expectations above already proved \
each of our generators sits inside this group with the right sign, so the two \
descriptions are the same stabilizer state written in two bases."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1521685512,ExpressionUUID->"023f49e2-a747-9723-33c5-60cfd2f3fa09"],

Cell["Part IV: A Parameter Stim Cannot Carry", "Section",
 CellID->97201263,ExpressionUUID->"21aa1c49-e16c-f406-57a9-9612901edf6c"],

Cell["\<\
Everything so far was a concrete code at a concrete size. The \
exact-and-symbolic corner has one more move, the one that has no numeric \
counterpart: hold a parameter symbolic and certify a whole family, or a whole \
continuum, at once.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->878831761,ExpressionUUID->"289b85cd-827f-9a9c-58b8-1c97370be8e6"],

Cell["A Family Indexed by n", "Subsection",
 CellID->1048559713,ExpressionUUID->"12f3dbee-abc4-6375-4280-d2cbce714e5f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Take the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["[",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["[",
     SpanMaxSize->1], 
    RowBox[{
     StyleBox["n", "TI"], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["n", "TI"], "-", "2"}], ",", "2"}], 
    StyleBox["]",
     SpanMaxSize->1]}], 
   StyleBox["]",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7ed5da6f-a344-b859-26ea-dc48d6f60aa3"],
 " code family: two generators, an all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2cfa2c34-ef50-7d46-5499-c6718ae547ac"],
 " string and an all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "362a4e41-4988-041f-5e2a-602b683d6f65"],
 " string, on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "01ed6b92-5a5f-fa78-3792-82dc5febc942"],
 " qubits. The generators must commute, and their symplectic product is a sum \
of contributions, one per qubit; the all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "107fa29c-d013-1000-4004-3bd9e1a6636a"],
 " and all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6798df62-4cc7-e4e1-0da7-d74c6573cecb"],
 " strings overlap on every qubit, so each of the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "49f5450b-e8d1-97dd-7f8b-5d548965fd27"],
 " qubits contributes a 1:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->300048012,ExpressionUUID->"2570a8a5-7a6f-59e7-530f-410f7f1a2bcd"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"omegaXZ", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Sum", "[", 
   RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"i", ",", " ", "n"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[18]:= ",
 CellID->524307849,ExpressionUUID->"0b990c23-b656-01a6-39a6-a58cc3e3538c"],

Cell[BoxData["n"], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[18]= ",
 CellID->1834301690,ExpressionUUID->"52243646-2fbd-210e-03db-a8204a487054"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The product is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1acd7f4d-3da3-7753-4af3-7717b4565db9"],
 ", carried symbolically. The two generators commute exactly when that count \
is even, which is the family's defining constraint: an odd ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "062d91f0-6326-41c0-2fd2-08ba16d3132a"],
 " would make the two generators anticommute, and there would be no code at \
all."
}], "Text",
 CellID->588230824,ExpressionUUID->"1ac7f74a-ce09-be58-4afc-ef17ebbd04a2"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Simplify", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Mod", "[", 
     RowBox[{"omegaXZ", ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], " ", "==", " ", "0"}], ",", 
   " ", 
   RowBox[{"Element", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"n", "/", "2"}], ",", " ", "Integers"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]], "Input",\

 CellLabel->"In[19]:= ",
 CellID->1127727415,ExpressionUUID->"6f6ad260-8ee1-565a-1569-d44a2a943ca0"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[19]= ",
 CellID->1955467569,ExpressionUUID->"612fd791-c81e-86f2-10d4-cedae9a8ecd8"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now the distance. Every weight-one error is detected, because a single ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["X", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "26dca2d1-2715-746d-4ee3-3b9b52a05eb7"],
 " overlaps the all-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "296a77cd-8fa3-dd44-5f6a-6e972a57a7ae"],
 " generator on exactly one qubit, an odd overlap, so it anticommutes; that \
rules out distance one for every ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6b853673-18d0-d9fa-19ba-a83d996aabc0"],
 ". A weight-two logical, on the other hand, commutes with both generators \
and is lighter than every nontrivial group element (each of which has weight ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "25b5b884-ef74-61b3-534a-31e18a013399"],
 ") precisely when two is less than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "574a2197-51ef-7938-7d49-b8d1679a4802"],
 ":"
}], "Text",
 CellID->512383039,ExpressionUUID->"6e85e9ed-427b-8528-16ba-00b7770fec72"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Reduce", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"2", " ", "<", " ", "n"}], ",", " ", "n", ",", " ", "Integers"}], 
  "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[20]:= ",
 CellID->688684714,ExpressionUUID->"73b76025-96bb-3fff-214d-798f234f85c5"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"n", "\[Element]", 
   TemplateBox[{},
    "Integers"]}], "&&", 
  RowBox[{"n", "\[GreaterEqual]", "3"}]}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[20]= ",
 CellID->1478107962,ExpressionUUID->"31f4c073-8ff6-126f-678b-da3d298360b5"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "So the distance is exactly two for the whole even-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "72ff7667-e560-b2c0-2285-68408c1a002a"],
 " family, established from a single evaluation with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7c9be80e-0554-61c1-24b8-0268ace1332b"],
 " symbolic. A numeric simulator has to instantiate a specific ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "106b7ad8-6378-599b-4069-7382160d2be1"],
 " to say anything at all; it can confirm any number of cases but never the \
family. As a bridge between the symbolic certificate and the concrete \
package, here is the package's numeric distance at several sizes, each an \
independent brute-force search, all landing where the certificate says they \
must:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1809098798,ExpressionUUID->"7e6f7c7a-fa8f-2da1-2615-7627ff3a778b"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"CodeDistance", "[", 
    RowBox[{"DistanceTwoCode", "[", "n", "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"n", ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"4", ",", " ", "6", ",", " ", "8", ",", " ", "10"}], "}"}]}], 
    "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[21]:= ",
 CellID->308320186,ExpressionUUID->"304452a4-201b-d64c-607c-4b0e51afbc96"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"2", ",", "2", ",", "2", ",", "2"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[21]= ",
 CellID->845252322,ExpressionUUID->"6beff1f8-c64a-83a6-1994-e8a5f3fcd18c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The same style of one-shot argument handles the repetition family ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["[",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["[",
     SpanMaxSize->1], 
    RowBox[{
     StyleBox["n", "TI"], ",", 
     RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}]}], 
    StyleBox["]",
     SpanMaxSize->1]}], 
   StyleBox["]",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4b5b9263-9199-4e16-7978-144d202c157c"],
 ": every generator is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "475d7054-346c-a3cc-6d63-6a1ebd1ef116"],
 "-type, so a lone ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2ef71510-aa09-eca9-568c-8d5dcfbdb6f3"],
 " commutes with all of them for every ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3343f580-98d5-e31c-6170-ecea1961ce66"],
 "; and a parity functional (the mod-2 sum of the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "20a7420b-376a-b23f-505d-6455421c0105"],
 "-support) vanishes on every weight-two generator but equals one on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6af863a9-1098-c609-1a98-7af3a112ad53"],
 ", so ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "055bc2ad-6b92-a3be-3378-dbf70e24f184"],
 " is never in the group. A single undetectable ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Z", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2b0fb60e-2211-365f-5934-286857a59ca5"],
 ", for every ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "201a2509-bdd9-2fa1-5039-bd53346c758b"],
 ": quantum distance one, the honest surprise of Part II, now stated for the \
whole family in one breath."
}], "Text",
 CellID->724760089,ExpressionUUID->"7085d256-6adc-aa08-20ba-d4100f6ef952"],

Cell["A Continuous Error Angle", "Subsection",
 CellID->168408080,ExpressionUUID->"5f932952-49a5-d34f-05a0-c11c6851a195"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Symbolic generality is not only about integer families; it reaches \
continuous parameters too, which is where a Pauli-sampling simulator has \
nothing to say. Consider a ",
 StyleBox["coherent", "TI"],
 " error on the bit-flip codeword: not a discrete bit flip, but a rotation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["R", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["x", "TI"]], 
   StyleBox["(",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[")",
    SpanMaxSize->1], "=", 
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["e", "TI"], 
    RowBox[{"-", 
     StyleBox["i", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["X", "TI"], "/", "2"}]]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "160f17c4-d31a-fe19-3e34-8ea1e6acc563"],
 " on the first qubit, through a symbolic angle. The state stays a \
superposition of \"no error\" and \"flipped,\" and the syndrome operator ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "1"], 
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "2"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "10beb8bd-bdb6-ee93-4045-31e73440b4f9"],
 " has an expectation value that is an exact function of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3fdc991b-fead-14b0-65e3-1144fb587e9a"],
 ":"
}], "Text",
 CellID->811283638,ExpressionUUID->"3657d2c5-05c0-ac85-5e6c-cbafac7af6ef"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"psi0", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"QuantumState", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"SparseArray", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"1", " ", "->", " ", "1"}], "}"}], ",", " ", "8"}], "]"}], ",",
      " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", " ", "2", ",", " ", "2"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"vTheta", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Normal", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"QuantumCircuitOperator", "[", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"\"\<RX\>\"", "[", "\[Theta]", "]"}], " ", "->", " ", "1"}], 
        "}"}], "]"}], "[", "psi0", "]"}], "[", "\"\<StateVector\>\"", "]"}], 
    "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"zzOp", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"KroneckerProduct", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"PauliMatrix", "[", "3", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"PauliMatrix", "[", "3", "]"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"IdentityMatrix", "[", "2", "]"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"zzExpectation", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Simplify", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Conjugate", "[", "vTheta", "]"}], " ", ".", " ", "zzOp", " ", ".",
      " ", "vTheta"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"\[Theta]", " ", "\[Element]", " ", "Reals"}]}], 
   "]"}]}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[22]:= ",
 CellID->1797514155,ExpressionUUID->"0642d375-09f3-9942-2e71-cb3faf87eba8"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Cos", "[", "\[Theta]", "]"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[22]= ",
 CellID->1258427959,ExpressionUUID->"0651775d-2bad-791b-2e6f-6f074e584861"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The syndrome expectation is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["cos",
    FontSlant->"Plain"], "\[ThinSpace]", 
   StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "63b9e3d6-2298-231e-1146-fa9057128e64"],
 ": it interpolates continuously from +1 at ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], "=", "0"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "67c13665-f813-2f11-0dfe-a84ef9a6727b"],
 " (no error, the codeword is untouched) to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4c440104-ea8a-4285-747b-99618f3f10ef"],
 " at ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], "=", "\[Pi]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "66c9a615-af45-c085-0ef6-385ecaf192ef"],
 " (a full bit flip, a definite nonzero syndrome), passing through zero at ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"], "=", "\[Pi]", "/", "2"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0513e607-0f4c-1e20-3320-f861aaf9650a"],
 " (a maximally uncertain syndrome). A coherent rotation is non-Clifford, so \
it lives in the exact dense simulator, not in a stabilizer tableau; carrying \
the angle symbolically returns the whole error curve as a closed form. This \
is the natural language for coherent-error and threshold questions, and it is \
exactly the representation a discrete stabilizer sampler cannot produce."
}], "Text",
 CellID->437474228,ExpressionUUID->"0371ff35-cd30-cda2-310e-f77ee4ca9788"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"zzExpectation", " ", "/.", " ", 
    RowBox[{"\[Theta]", " ", "->", " ", "0"}]}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"zzExpectation", " ", "/.", " ", 
    RowBox[{"\[Theta]", " ", "->", " ", 
     RowBox[{"Pi", "/", "2"}]}]}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"zzExpectation", " ", "/.", " ", 
    RowBox[{"\[Theta]", " ", "->", " ", "Pi"}]}]}], "}"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[23]:= ",
 CellID->590983534,ExpressionUUID->"6833ae6a-6ac5-a2c8-19c0-40340f71f012"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1", ",", "0", ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", "1"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellLabel->"Out[23]= ",
 CellID->1257758076,ExpressionUUID->"5f7f2999-1aee-25a5-0504-c0c39e988f8f"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The three sampled values confirm the endpoints and the midpoint of the closed \
form we just derived.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->11631581,ExpressionUUID->"128c7526-c883-085d-42b4-6c83e9365aa7"],

Cell["Part V: Where This Leaves Us", "Section",
 CellID->1933762060,ExpressionUUID->"3279c00f-c9e2-8758-6206-da68ef94eda2"],

Cell["\<\
Before we close, let us summarize what we have actually established:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1055776787,ExpressionUUID->"50b12ff8-e606-85f7-004e-c6a593b0efdd"],

Cell["\<\
For the bit-flip, phase-flip, Shor, five-qubit, and Steane codes, the exact \
code distance from a from-scratch symplectic search matches the package's \
distance in every case.\
\>", "Item",
 CellID->1859711813,ExpressionUUID->"640f47a5-4648-c62d-0c35-5e0f73e2ad77"],

Cell["\<\
The package's minimum-weight witnesses are valid logicals of that weight, \
verified both by our rank-and-commutation test and by Stim's \
undetectable-and-nontrivial test.\
\>", "Item",
 CellID->1348896709,ExpressionUUID->"63a48922-0fac-666c-115b-208caa594cb6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Each code's logical operators satisfy the full commutation algebra (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    StyleBox["X", "TI"], "_"], 
   StyleBox["i", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "387a9e0d-0234-0e73-6819-1057b7c15559"],
 " anticommutes with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   OverscriptBox[
    StyleBox["Z", "TI"], "_"], 
   StyleBox["i", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3f64ca0d-9927-5f44-651b-ec5718d225ae"],
 " and commutes with everything else), checked independently."
}], "Item",
 CellID->1427211115,ExpressionUUID->"399d1ff3-4632-be92-6fa2-96bd73c404f8"],

Cell["\<\
Each encoder prepares a sign-exact +1 codeword, confirmed both by dense Pauli \
matrices and by Stim's tableau expectation values.\
\>", "Item",
 CellID->2099805207,ExpressionUUID->"1170384d-2f38-2c50-470f-b2174acd76ba"],

Cell["\<\
The package's syndrome equals Stim's across every weight-one and weight-two \
error, code by code.\
\>", "Item",
 CellID->1402006442,ExpressionUUID->"6ce33075-6d34-44a7-1490-aa3f04c12e8d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["[",
    SpanMaxSize->1], 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["[",
     SpanMaxSize->1], 
    RowBox[{
     StyleBox["n", "TI"], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["n", "TI"], "-", "2"}], ",", "2"}], 
    StyleBox["]",
     SpanMaxSize->1]}], 
   StyleBox["]",
    SpanMaxSize->1]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "54e2b6d5-0ba4-5b19-7c91-2f9fae512663"],
 " and repetition families are certified at symbolic ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6334ac9c-43f1-85c0-12cb-45c67585ef2a"],
 " in one evaluation, and a coherent error's syndrome is returned as the \
exact function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["cos",
    FontSlant->"Plain"], "\[ThinSpace]", 
   StyleBox["\[Theta]", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "74397683-04f4-1d4d-1dc7-6fedac816797"],
 "."
}], "Item",
 CellID->1901179466,ExpressionUUID->"735e49d5-32a1-4a48-2166-609f47541892"],

Cell["\<\
The picture that leaves us with is a clean division of labor. Stim owns scale \
and speed: sampling large circuits, benchmarking decoders, estimating \
thresholds. This exact layer owns the small-and-certain corner: the exact \
minimum distance, a witness for it, the logical algebra, a sign-exact \
encoder, and, uniquely, the ability to hold a parameter symbolic and certify \
an entire family or a continuous error at once. The two agree wherever they \
overlap, which is what makes the exact answers trustworthy and the symbolic \
ones worth reaching for.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->210724908,ExpressionUUID->"335d55a2-cf9b-493d-6163-4c0fea2e1807"],

Cell["\<\
The honest boundary is worth restating: exact minimum distance is NP-hard, so \
this is a small-code instrument by mathematical necessity, not a scaling \
competitor. That is the right trade to have made. When you want the exact \
answer for a textbook code, or a statement about a whole family, the \
exactness and the symbol are the point; when you want a threshold for a \
distance-25 surface code, reach for Stim. Both live in the same notebook, and \
now you can check one against the other yourself.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1992790374,ExpressionUUID->"3bfbbd45-edb8-1b0b-6998-352e844d6651"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If you want to push further: feed your own stabilizer generators into ",
 Cell[BoxData["QECCode"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "045023ab-c37d-b770-2c6f-baf4f6091a5a"],
 ", run the same independent checks, and diff against Stim; or take the \
symbolic-family argument to a code family of your own and see whether one \
evaluation can settle it."
}], "Text",
 CellID->292747604,ExpressionUUID->"73e8aeff-5b91-f8f4-2197-47b95e25cade"]
},
